[THOUGHT]  
- Red Flag symptoms (fever, bilious vomiting, lethargy, pallor, decreased wet diapers, abdominal rigidity, inconsolable crying) are all explicitly absent — reassuring.  
- Critical tests (urinalysis, urine culture, DRE) are completed and unremarkable; hydration status confirmed normal.  
- Abdominal exam is thoroughly characterized: soft, distended, tympanitic (LLQ), non-tender, with normal bowel sounds — inconsistent with obstruction, peritonitis, or significant constipation.  
- The proposed diagnosis — *Benign Feeding Aversion / Developmental Phase* — is fully consistent with *all* key findings:  
  • Age-appropriate (8 months: peak period for feeding transitions, oral motor development, emerging food preferences, and transient aversions);  
  • Absence of objective pathology across infectious, GI, GU, ENT, and neurologic domains;  
  • Nonspecific symptoms (fussiness + reduced feeding) without red flags align precisely with this benign, self-limited hypothesis;  
  • No finding contradicts it — notably, the absence of stooling changes, teething signs, respiratory symptoms, or systemic illness strengthens rather than weakens this diagnosis.  
- While other differentials (e.g., subclinical viral illness, functional GI disorder) remain *possible*, none are *better supported*:  
  • Viral prodrome lacks any supportive sign (no rhinorrhea, no low-grade temp, no rash/conjunctivitis);  
  • Functional constipation is undermined by explicit absence of straining, stooling change, or palpable stool;  
  • Intussusception and metabolic disease are strongly contradicted by absence of classic or even subtle alarm features (e.g., no episodic pain, no pallor/lethargy, no abnormal tone, no acidosis/ketosis on UA).  
- Turn limit is reached (10/10), and no further data can be gathered within protocol. The evidence is sufficient to conclude the most likely diagnosis — not merely “not ruled out”, but *positively supported* by pattern concordance and negative exclusion.

[DECISION] PASS

[FEEDBACK]

